UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 2: Teeth, hands and skin care', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy. Teeth: brush for two minutes twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, spit out the toothpaste without rinsing, limit sugary food and drinks and visit the dentist. Hands: wash, dry and keep nails short and clean. Skin: protect it from the sun, keep it clean, cover cuts with a clean plaster and tell an adult if a cut or rash looks sore or red.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a two-minute tooth-brushing plan that covers all the teeth, with the parts of the mouth in order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the chewing surfaces, then a gentle brush of the tongue. Spit but do not rinse so the fluoride stays on the teeth.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty. Brushing twice a day removes the sticky plaque that causes holes in teeth.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a one-week care chart for teeth, hands and skin with morning, afternoon and night tasks. Tick it each day, time two tooth-brushing sessions and write what you would do if you had a small cut and who you would tell.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy. Teeth: brush for two minutes twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, spit out the toothpaste without rinsing, limit sugary food and drinks and visit the dentist. Hands: wash, dry and keep nails short and clean. Skin: protect it from the sun, keep it clean, cover cuts with a clean plaster and tell an adult if a cut or rash looks sore or red.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a one-week care chart for teeth, hands and skin with morning, afternoon and night tasks. Tick it each day, time two tooth-brushing sessions and write what you would do if you had a small cut and who you would tell. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using tooth brushing on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty.. Correction: Sample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty. Brushing twice a day removes the sticky plaque that causes holes in teeth. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating tooth brushing on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with tooth brushing, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about tooth brushing with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for tooth brushing. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for tooth brushing. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Sample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the"],"question":"Write a two-minute tooth-brushing plan that covers all the teeth, with the parts of the mouth in order. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for tooth brushing; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy","Sample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the","Careful use of hand care and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with tooth brushing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named hand care and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty","Name hand care and explain why it matters only, without linking it to tooth brushing","Apply tooth brushing to new material and name the hand care and explain why it matters used"],"question":"Which response best applies tooth brushing with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on tooth brushing. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty","Sample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for tooth brushing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with tooth brushing","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about tooth brushing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example daily care routine","Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tooth brushing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for tooth brushing?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty","Name hand care and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Make a one-week care chart for teeth, hands and skin with morning, afternoon and night tasks","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies tooth brushing to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about tooth brushing with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating tooth brushing on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using tooth brushing on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for tooth brushing. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with tooth brushing, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty.. Correction: Sample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w39-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise tooth brushing.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use tooth brushing with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify hand care and explain why it matters.","I can practise skin care independently on a new prompt.","I can notice telling an adult needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tooth brushing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample daily care routine. Morning: brush teeth for 2 minutes (a timer helps) and put sunscreen on uncovered skin. Afternoon: wash hands before snacks. Night: brush teeth, spit and do not rinse, then look at hands and nails. When Ben gets a scrape he rinses it, tells an adult and covers it with a clean plaster.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a two-minute tooth-brushing plan that covers all the teeth, with the parts of the mouth in order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the chewing surfaces, then a gentle brush of the tongue. Spit but do not rinse so the fluoride stays on the teeth.","explain":"Looking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy. Teeth: brush for two minutes twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, spit out the toothpaste without rinsing, limit sugary food and drinks and visit the dentist. Hands: wash, dry and keep nails short and clean. Skin: protect it from the sun, keep it clean, cover cuts with a clean plaster and tell an adult if a cut or rash looks sore or red.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty. Brushing twice a day removes the sticky plaque that causes holes in teeth. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Tooth brushing.","caption":"Spit, do not rinse.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Teeth</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 minutes, twice a day</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Spit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">do not rinse</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example daily care routine. Morning: brush teeth for 2 minutes (a timer helps) and put sunscreen on uncovered skin. Afte Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example daily care routine. Morning: brush teeth for 2 minutes (a timer helps) and put sunscreen on uncovered skin. Afte Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tooth brushing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample daily care routine. Morning: brush teeth for 2 minutes (a timer helps) and put sunscreen on uncovered skin. Afternoon: wash hands before snacks. Night: brush teeth, spit and do not rinse, then look at hands and nails. When Ben gets a scrape he rinses it, tells an adult and covers it with a clean plaster. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Skin and hand care.","caption":"Tell an adult.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Skin</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sunscreen, clean plaster</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Tell</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">an adult if red or sore</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tooth brushing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty. Brushing twice a day removes the sticky plaque that causes holes in teeth. Here is a more helpful example: Example daily care routine. Morning: brush teeth for 2 minutes (a timer helps) and put sunscreen on uncovered skin. Afternoon: wash hands before snacks. Night: brush teeth, spit and do not rinse, then look at hands and nails. When Ben gets a scrape he rinses it, tells an adult and covers it with a clean plaster.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: 30 seconds for each of four areas (top outside, top inside, bottom outside, bottom inside), including the chewing surfaces, then a gentle brush of the tongue. Spit but do not rinse so the fluoride stays on the teeth.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Thinking you only need to brush when your teeth feel dirty. Brushing twice a day removes the sticky plaque that causes holes in teeth. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no reason","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about tooth brushing with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nLooking after teeth, hands and skin keeps us healthy. Teeth: brush for two minutes twice a day with fluoride toothpaste, spit out the toothpaste without rinsing, limit sugary food and drinks and visit the dentist. Hands: wash, dry and keep nails short and clean. Skin: protect it from the sun, keep it clean, cover cuts with a clean plaster and tell an adult if a cut or rash looks sore or red. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 39, Lesson 2: Teeth, hands and skin care","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":39,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample daily care routine. Morning: brush teeth for 2 minutes (a timer helps) and put sunscreen on uncovered skin. Afternoon: wash hands before snacks. Night: brush teeth, spit and do not rinse, then look at hands and nails. When Ben gets a scrape he rinses it, tells an adult and covers it with a clean plaster.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Tooth brushing.","caption":"Spit, do not rinse.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Teeth</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">2 minutes, twice a day</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Spit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">do not rinse</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Skin and hand care.","caption":"Tell an adult.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" r